{
  "requirement": "Repository validation MUST continue after a failed check and report every failed\ncheck it finds in one run, each as a {term}`requirements.ci.validation_error`.\nThis is a convention for every validation error; the requirement for each check\nstates which conditions that check reports. A file that cannot be parsed, such as\na malformed `habeas.toml`, `book.toml`, or evidence file, is one failed check:\nits error MAY report only the first parse or decoding problem in that file, and\nchecks that do not depend on its contents MUST still run.\n\nThe code that sequences the repository's checks carries out the first paragraph.\nA repository-validation function that collects validation errors MUST hold them\nin a parameter or local variable named `errors`, and a function with such a\nvariable is a site below; authoring code that collects errors only to abort is\nnot one. After one of its checks fails, a site MUST still run every later check\nthat does not depend on the failed check's result, including later checks in the\nsame loop iteration, and MUST NOT report one failure twice.\n\n::::{sites} error_accumulators\n```yaml\nlanguage: rust\npaths: [src, crates/trust-but-verify/src]\nexclude: [src/authoring/inputs.rs, src/authoring/tools.rs]\nrule:\n  kind: function_item\n  has:\n    stopBy: { kind: function_item }\n    any:\n      - kind: parameter\n        has: { field: pattern, regex: \"^errors$\" }\n      - kind: let_declaration\n        has: { field: pattern, regex: \"^errors$\" }\n  not:\n    inside:\n      stopBy: end\n      kind: mod_item\n      has: { field: name, regex: \"^tests$\" }\nmatch:\n  - \"fn check(value: &str, errors: &mut Vec<String>) {}\"\n  - |\n    fn run() {\n        let mut errors: BTreeSet<String> = BTreeSet::new();\n        check(\"\", &mut errors);\n    }\nno_match:\n  - \"fn check(value: &str, discovery_errors: &mut Vec<String>) {}\"\n  - |\n    fn run() {\n        let mut problems = Vec::new();\n        check(\"\", &mut problems);\n    }\n  - |\n    mod tests {\n        fn check(errors: &mut Vec<String>) {}\n    }\n```\n::::\n\n::::{violations} early_exits\n```yaml\nlanguage: rust\npaths: [src, crates/trust-but-verify/src]\nwithin: error_accumulators\nrule:\n  any:\n    - kind: try_expression\n    - kind: return_expression\n    - kind: continue_expression\n    - kind: break_expression\nmatch:\n  - |\n    fn check(v: &Value, errors: &mut Vec<String>) -> Option<u8> {\n        let n = v.as_u64()?;\n        if v.is_null() { errors.push(\"null\".into()); }\n        Some(n as u8)\n    }\n  - |\n    fn check(items: &[Value], errors: &mut Vec<String>) {\n        for item in items {\n            if !item.is_object() { errors.push(\"object\".into()); continue; }\n            if item.is_null() { errors.push(\"null\".into()); }\n        }\n    }\n  - |\n    fn check(v: &Value, errors: &mut Vec<String>) {\n        if !v.is_object() { errors.push(\"object\".into()); return; }\n        if v.is_null() { errors.push(\"null\".into()); }\n    }\n  - |\n    fn check(items: &[Value], errors: &mut Vec<String>) {\n        for item in items {\n            if !item.is_object() { errors.push(\"object\".into()); break; }\n        }\n        if items.is_empty() { errors.push(\"empty\".into()); }\n    }\nno_match:\n  - |\n    fn parse(v: &Value) -> Option<u8> {\n        let n = v.as_u64()?;\n        Some(n as u8)\n    }\n```\n::::",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "I judge the tests, site query and violation query adequate for `requirements.ci.errors`.\n\n**Obligations and where they are checked**\n- **Continue after a failed check and report every failure in one run.** The sequencing code is `inspect_repository_with_progress` and `validate_repository`. These tests cover it:\n  - `failed_checks_do_not_hide_later_independent_checks` (`src/evidence.rs`) combines invalid names, bad inputs, a missing file, and misplaced or inline `{fixme}` references. It asserts that every expected error appears.\n  - `configuration_and_dependency_failures_do_not_stop_later_checks` (`src/evidence.rs`) covers unresolved terms and a bad definition input, and shows other records are still checked.\n  - `collects_missing_orphan_format_false_and_changed_line_errors` (`src/evidence.rs`) checks a long list of independent errors from one run.\n  - `schema_rejects_each_malformed_field_and_input_form` (`src/evidence.rs`) checks each malformed field of an evidence record and input.\n  - `git_index_failure_is_reported_once_and_later_checks_still_run` (`src/evidence.rs`) fails reference discovery and shows later evidence checks still run.\n  - `malformed_citation_entries_do_not_hide_later_independent_errors` and `unclosed_fences_still_report_unknown_names` (`tests/references.rs`) cover citation-review and reference checking.\n  - `accepted_exceptions_cannot_waive_invalid_measurement_and_checks_still_collect_errors` (`src/execution_coverage/check.rs`) covers coverage checking.\n- **A parse failure is one failed check, and independent checks still run.** The modes named in the text are a malformed `habeas.toml`, a malformed `book.toml`, and a malformed evidence file.\n  - `configuration_and_dependency_failures_do_not_stop_later_checks` writes all three. They are `version = 2`, a truncated `book.toml`, and `evidence/ghost.json` containing `{`. It asserts that an unknown-reference error in `src/main.rs` is still reported.\n  - `status_loads_nearest_workspace_file_without_validating_authoring` (`tests/commands.rs`) shows an invalid `habeas.toml` still lets the malformed evidence file be reported, through `status` and `info`.\n  - `inspection_progress_preserves_validation_and_finishes_each_phase` (`src/evidence.rs`) covers a malformed evidence file together with an unparsable Rust source.\n- **No failure reported twice.** These tests assert it:\n  - `failed_checks_do_not_hide_later_independent_checks` sorts and dedups the errors and compares counts.\n  - `configuration_and_dependency_failures_do_not_stop_later_checks` counts the `habeas.toml` and `book.toml` messages and checks that no error is duplicated.\n  - The Git-index test, `an_unreadable_record_is_reported_once` (`src/index.rs`) and `an_unlistable_review_directory_is_reported_once` (`tests/evidence_boundaries.rs`) each assert a single occurrence.\n  - `every_review_kind_loads_both_formats_and_reports_each_application_failure_once` (`src/authoring/sdk.rs`) asserts that findings are unique.\n  - `failed_site_queries_do_not_repeat_errors_through_dependent_violations` (`src/sites.rs`) covers the same property for site-query failures.\n- **The `errors` naming convention and each site's own continue-after-failure behaviour.** The text assigns these to each site's own review, so I did not vet individual sites.\n\n**Site query `error_accumulators`**\nI ran it through `ast_grep`.\n- **Matches.** It selects functions that take an `errors` parameter or declare `let errors`. Examples are `DiscoverySources::files` and `walk`, the `citations.rs` validators, `inspect_repository_with_progress`, and the `book_source_dir`, `collect_definitions`, `validate_evidence_file` and `validate_input` functions in `src/evidence.rs`. `validate_queries` in `src/sites.rs` also matches, because it has an `errors` parameter. Its local `problems` vector is per-query scratch, not the accumulator.\n- **Exclusions.** The excluded `src/authoring/inputs.rs` and `src/authoring/tools.rs` are the authoring code that collects errors only to abort. I searched `src/authoring` for other non-test uses of `errors` and found none outside `policy.rs`.\n- **Differently named collectors.** The ones I found are abort-only or not validation sites:\n  - `load_definitions` is commented as aborting on any failure.\n  - The `failures` and `diagnostics` locals in `src/evidence.rs`, `src/index.rs` and `src/execution_coverage` hold nothing the requirement obliges to be named `errors`.\n- **Snippets.** The match snippets cover a parameter and a `let` binding. The no_match snippets cover a `discovery_errors` parameter, a `problems` local, and a `mod tests`. Together they exercise each condition in the query.\n\n**Violation query `early_exits`**\nIt has a match snippet for each construct it flags: `?`, `continue`, `return`, and `break`. Each snippet has a later check that the early exit would skip. The no_match snippet is a function with no `errors` accumulator. It also only runs `within` the sites. As a heuristic it may miss early-exit forms such as `panic!`, or exits hidden inside a closure. That is a note and not a gap.\n\nNo test-adequacy gap or site-query gap was found, and there are no acknowledgments.",
  "references": "dd20f8da82496be36c29b330ae5a79b4902435b148f846b9f0ac46fe08864733",
